## Appendix A Conserved Noether currents

We present a discussion of important Noether currents of the Lagrangian \(\mathcal{L}\) of Eq. (1), i.e., the conservation laws deriving from translation and rotation invariance. Whereas these conservation laws are manifest spin-gauge invariant, the corresponding canonical energy-momentum tensor and angular momentum vector current themselves depend on the spin-gauge potential.[58; 59; 60; 61] In general, this precludes the definition of a conserved total canonical momentum and angular momentum. However, in case the topological charge is preserved within the sample a definition of a conserved total momentum and angular momentum becomes possible in agreement with previous findings by Papanicolaou and Tomaras.[60] For the indices we use the conventions of Table 2.

### Canonical energy-momentum tensor

The theory (1) is translationally invariant so that the canonical energy momentum tensor

\[T_{\mu\nu}=\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial(d_{\mu}\hat{n})}d_{\nu}\hat{n} -\delta_{\mu\nu}\mathcal{L} \tag{36}\]

is conserved

\[d_{\mu}T_{\mu\nu}=0. \tag{37}\]

The tensor \(T_{\mu\nu}\) depends on the spin-gauge field \(\vec{A}\) of Eq. (3). For example, the canonical momentum density

\[T_{0\alpha}=-\frac{\hbar}{a^{2}}\vec{A}d_{\alpha}\hat{n} \tag{38}\]

is determined by \(\vec{A}\).[58] Nevertheless, the conservation law (37) itself is gauge invariant. This is best seen by first defining the energy-momentum tensor deriving from the static Lagrangian \(\mathcal{L}_{\rm stat}\) only,

\[T_{\mu\nu}^{\rm stat}=\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{\rm stat}}{\partial(d_{\mu} \hat{n})}d_{\nu}\hat{n}-\delta_{\mu\nu}\mathcal{L}_{\rm stat}. \tag{39}\]

The conservation law (37) can then be written in the manifest spin-gauge invariant form

\[d_{\mu}T_{\mu\nu}^{\rm stat}-\frac{4\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\epsilon_{\mu 0\nu}j_{\mu}^{\rm top }=0, \tag{40}\]

where we have used the identity of Eq. (8). So we arrive at the result that the divergence of \(T_{\mu\nu}^{\rm stat}\) is given by the topological current of Eq. (4).

Assuming that the integral over the spatial divergence \(\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}d_{\alpha}T_{\alpha\mu}^{\rm stat}=0\), i.e., that \(T_{\alpha\mu}^{\rm stat}\) vanishes on the surface of the two-dimensional sample, it follows from the time-component of Eq. (40) the conservation of total energy, \(d_{0}E=0\), with

\[E\equiv-\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}T_{00}^{\rm stat}=\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\mathcal{L}_{\rm stat}. \tag{41}\]

Due to the anomalous form of the conservation law, however, we are in general not able to define a conserved total momentum. Instead it follows from the spatial component of (40) with \(T_{0\alpha}^{\rm stat}=0\) that the spatial integral over the topological current vanishes

\[-\frac{4\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\epsilon_{\alpha 0\beta}\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,j_{ \alpha}^{\rm top}=0. \tag{42}\]

However, with the additional assumption that the conservation law for the topological current of Eq. (5) holds and that the product \(\mathbf{r}_{\alpha}j_{\beta}^{\rm top}\) vanishes on the surface, we can write

\[\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}j_{\alpha}^{\rm top}=\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\left(-d_{ \beta}(\mathbf{r}_{\alpha}j_{\beta}^{\rm top})+j_{\alpha}^{\rm top}\right) \tag{43}\] \[=-\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\mathbf{r}_{\alpha}d_{\beta}j_{\beta}^{\rm top}= d_{0}\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\mathbf{r}_{\alpha}j_{0}^{\rm top}.\]

In this case, following Ref. [60] we can identify a total conserved momentum

\[\mathbf{P}_{\alpha}=-\frac{4\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\epsilon_{0\alpha\beta}\int d^{2}\mathbf{r }\,\,\mathbf{r}_{\beta}j_{0}^{\rm top} \tag{44}\]

with the first moment of the topological charge distribution.

In Ref. [60] this result for the conserved momentum was obtained by starting directly from the equation for the time-derivative of the topological charge density

\[\frac{4\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}d_{0}j_{0}^{\rm top}=\epsilon_{0\alpha\beta}d_{\alpha}d _{\gamma}T_{\gamma\beta}^{\rm stat}, \tag{45}\]

which can be derived from Eq. (40) by applying the derivative \(\epsilon_{\alpha 0\nu}d_{\alpha}\) with \(\alpha=1,2\), summing over \(\nu\), and using the conservation law for the topological current Eq. (5).

### Angular momentum

For completeness, we also discuss the conservation law deriving from rotational invariance. Due to the spin-orbit coupling \(Q\), only the total angular momentum vector current obeys a conservation law. In the following, we first discuss the spin-, afterwards the orbital and, finally, the total angular momentum.

Spin angular momentumFrom the rotations by an infinitesimal angle \(\omega\) of the magnetisation around the magnetic field direction \(\hat{B}=\hat{z}\), \(\hat{n}_{i}\rightarrow\hat{n}_{i}+\delta\hat{n}_{i}\) with

\[\delta\hat{n}_{i}=\omega\epsilon_{i\pm}\hat{n}_{k} \tag{108}\]

follows

\[d_{\mu}\mathcal{S}_{\mu} =-\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{\rm stat}}{\partial n_{i}}\epsilon_{ i\pm}\hat{n}_{k}-\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{\rm stat}}{\partial d_{\mu}\hat{n}_{i}} \epsilon_{i\pm k}d_{\mu}\hat{n}_{k} \tag{109}\] \[=-\varepsilon_{0}Q(\hat{n}_{z}d_{\alpha}\hat{n}_{\alpha}-\hat{n} _{\alpha}d_{\alpha}\hat{n}_{z}).\]

The 2+1 spin angular momentum current,

\[\mathcal{S}_{0} =-\frac{\hbar}{a^{2}}\hat{n}_{z}, \tag{110}\] \[\mathcal{S}_{\alpha} =-\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{\rm stat}}{\partial d_{\alpha}\hat{n }_{i}}\epsilon_{i\pm}\hat{n}_{k}\] (111) \[=-\varepsilon_{0}[\epsilon_{i\pm}\hat{n}_{k}\hat{n}_{k}d_{\alpha }\hat{n}_{i}+Q(\hat{n}_{z}\hat{n}_{\alpha}-\delta_{z\alpha})]\]

is not conserved as the right-hand side of Eq. (109) is finite due to the spin-orbit coupling \(Q\). Note that according to our sign convention in Eq. (3) the magnetisation vector points antiparallel to the spin vector as it is for example the case for electrons.

Orbital angular momentumPerforming an infinitesimal orbital rotation around the \(z\)-axis

\[\delta\hat{n}_{i}=-\omega\epsilon_{\alpha 0\beta}\mathbf{r}_{\beta}d_{\alpha}\hat{n }_{i} \tag{112}\]

we obtain

\[d_{\mu}L_{\mu}=\epsilon_{\beta 0\alpha}T_{\alpha\beta}. \tag{113}\]

The angular momentum

\[L_{\mu}=\epsilon_{\beta 0\gamma}\mathbf{r}_{\gamma}T_{\mu\beta} \tag{114}\]

is not conserved because the energy-momentum tensor is not symmetric due to the spin-orbit coupling \(Q\).

Total angular momentumThe theory (1) is invariant with respect to a combined rotation of spin and real space around the magnetic field direction. As a result, the total angular momentum

\[J_{\mu}=\mathcal{S}_{\mu}+L_{\mu} \tag{115}\]

is conserved

\[d_{\mu}J_{\mu}=0, \tag{116}\]

which follows from Eqs. (109) and (113). Similarly as for the energy-momentum tensor, the conservation law (116) is spin-gauge invariant whereas \(J_{\mu}\) itself is not. Introducing the part of the angular momentum current

\[\tilde{J}_{\mu}=\mathcal{S}_{\mu}+\epsilon_{\beta 0\gamma}\mathbf{r}_{\gamma}T^{\rm stat}_ {\mu\beta} \tag{117}\]

that is manifest invariant, the conservation law assumes the form

\[d_{\mu}\tilde{J}_{\mu}+\frac{4\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\mathbf{r}_{\alpha}j^{\rm top}_{ \alpha}=0. \tag{118}\]

With the assumption that the current \(\tilde{J}_{\alpha}\) vanishes on the surface of the two-dimensional sample \(\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}d_{\alpha}\tilde{J}_{\alpha}=0\) we find

\[d_{0}\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\tilde{J}_{0}+\frac{4\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\, \mathbf{r}_{\alpha}j^{\rm top}_{\alpha}=0. \tag{119}\]

The time-dependence of the spatial integral over the density, \(\tilde{J}_{0}=-\frac{\hbar}{a^{2}}\hat{n}_{z}\), is thus determined by the integrated scalar product of the position vector \(\mathbf{r}\) with the spatial topological current \(j^{\rm top}_{\alpha}\). With the additional assumption that the topological current is conserved, Eq. (5), and that the product \(\mathbf{r}^{2}j^{\rm top}_{\alpha}\) vanishes on the surface, we can rewrite the last term as a time derivative

\[\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\mathbf{r}_{\alpha}j^{\rm top}_{\alpha}=\int d^{2} \mathbf{r}\,\frac{1}{2}\Big{(}d_{\alpha}(\mathbf{r}^{2}j^{\rm top}_{\alpha})-\mathbf{r}^{ 2}d_{\alpha}j^{\rm top}_{\alpha}\Big{)}\] \[=d_{0}\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{r}^{2}j^{\rm top}_{0}. \tag{120}\]

As a result, we finally obtain

\[\mathcal{J}=\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\left(-\frac{\hbar}{a^{2}}\hat{n}_{z}+\frac{2 \pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\mathbf{r}^{2}j^{\rm top}_{0}\right) \tag{121}\]

for the conserved total angular momentum, \(d_{0}\mathcal{J}=0\).[60]

### Momentum and angular momentum of the skyrmion

It is instructive to compute the momentum and the angular momentum attributed to the magnetic skyrmion solution of section II.2. Neglecting the massive fluctuations, \(\psi\), we obtain for the momentum (101)

\[\mathbf{P}^{\rm skyrmion}_{\alpha} =-\frac{4\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\epsilon_{0\alpha\beta}\int d^{2}\mathbf{r} \,\,\mathbf{r}_{\beta}j^{\rm top}_{0}(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{R})\Big{|}_{\psi,\psi^{\prime}=0}\] \[=\frac{4\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\epsilon_{0\alpha\beta}\mathbf{R}_{\beta}. \tag{122}\]

On this level of approximation, the conservation of \(\mathbf{P}^{\rm skyrmion}_{\alpha}\) directly follows from the equation of motion (24),

\[d_{0}\mathbf{P}^{\rm skyrmion}_{\alpha}=\frac{4\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\epsilon_{0\alpha\beta }d_{0}\mathbf{R}_{\beta}=(\mathbf{G}\times d_{0}\mathbf{R})_{\alpha}=0 \tag{123}\]

The angular momentum (121) of the fully field-polarized state, \(\hat{n}=\hat{z}\), is already non-zero and given by the total spin of the sample, \(\mathcal{J}_{\rm FP}=-\mathcal{V}\hbar/a^{2}\) where\(\mathcal{V}\) is the volume. Neglecting again the massive fluctuations, we find for the angular momentum attributed to the skyrmion

\[\mathcal{J}_{\rm skyr} =\mathcal{J}\Big{|}_{\psi,\psi^{*}=0}-\mathcal{J}_{\rm FP} \tag{100}\] \[=\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\left(-\frac{\hbar}{a^{2}}(\hat{n}_{z}-1)+ \frac{2\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\mathbf{r}^{2}j_{0}^{\rm top}\right)\Big{|}_{\psi,\psi^{*}=0}\] \[=\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\left(-\frac{\hbar}{a^{2}}(\hat{n}_{z}-1)+ \frac{2\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{R})^{2}j_{0}^{\rm top}\right)\Big{|}_{\psi, \psi^{*}=0}\] \[\quad+\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\left(\frac{2\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}(2\mathbf{r}\bm {R}-\mathbf{R}^{2})j_{0}^{\rm top}\right)\Big{|}_{\psi,\psi^{*}=0}\]

It turns out that the first line in the last equation exactly vanishes,

\[\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,\left(-\frac{\hbar}{a^{2}}(\hat{n}_{z}-1)+ \frac{2\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}(\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{R})^{2}j_{0}^{\rm top}\right)\Big{|}_{\psi, \psi^{*}=0}\] \[=\frac{2\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\int_{0}^{\infty}d\rho\,\rho\left(-( \cos\theta-1)+\frac{\rho}{2}\theta^{\prime}\sin\theta\right)\] \[=\frac{2\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\rho^{2}\sin^{2}\frac{\theta}{2}\Big{|}_{ \rho=0}^{\infty}=0, \tag{101}\]

due to the exponentially fast approach of the polar angle \(\theta\) to its boundary value at large distances, \(\theta\to 0\). The change of spin angular momentum due to the spatial dependence of \(\hat{n}_{z}(\mathbf{r})\) is thus exactly compensated by the orbital angular momentum that is carried by the skyrmion texture. The skyrmion angular momentum then reduces to

\[\mathcal{J}_{\rm skyr} =-\frac{2\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\,(2\mathbf{r}\mathbf{R}-\mathbf{R}^{ 2})j_{0}^{\rm top}\Big{|}_{\psi,\psi^{*}=0}\] \[=-\frac{2\pi\hbar}{a^{2}}\mathbf{R}^{2}. \tag{102}\]

The time derivative of \(\mathcal{J}_{\rm skyr}\), i.e., the torque assumes with the help of Eq. (100) the following intuitive form

\[d_{0}\mathcal{J}_{\rm skyr}=(\mathbf{R}\times d_{0}\mathbf{P}_{\rm skyr})_{z}. \tag{103}\]

